      "status": "PASS",
      "independent_solution": "A valid verification requires both a true power equation and the defining correspondence log_a b = c iff a^c = b. The stem's equalities are not treated as already-proved numerical facts. Evaluate each proposed power and preserve the original base, argument and exponent, including the minus sign.",
      "correct": ["A", "C", "D", "F"],
      "option_checks": [
        {"option_id": "A", "truth": "correct", "evidence": "3^2 = 3 * 3 = 9. Base 3, exponent 2 and result 9 correspond exactly to log_3(9) = 2; both required conditions hold."},
        {"option_id": "B", "truth": "incorrect", "evidence": "125^3 = 1,953,125, not 5. Moreover the source logarithm requires base 5 and argument 125, so its defining test is 5^3 = 125, not a base-125 power. This is false numerically and is the wrong correspondence."},
        {"option_id": "C", "truth": "correct", "evidence": "10^(-1) = 1/10. The proposed negative exponent, base and result match log_10(1/10) = -1."},
        {"option_id": "D", "truth": "correct", "evidence": "4^(1/2) is the positive square root of 4, namely 2. It is exactly the defining power for log_4(2) = 1/2."},
        {"option_id": "E", "truth": "incorrect", "evidence": "2^6 = 64, not 1/64. The source equality requires exponent -6, whose power is the reciprocal 2^(-6) = 1/64. The proposed equation changes the exponent sign and is false."},
        {"option_id": "F", "truth": "correct", "evidence": "(1/3)^(-4) = 3^4 = 81. A positive base between 0 and 1 is valid, and the negative exponent matches the source equality."}
      ],
      "complete_set_check": "All six alternatives examined. The four valid true defining-power checks are selected; the two false/noncorresponding checks are excluded."
    },
    {
      "view_index": 1,
      "view_sha256": "b0649e249410cfe894e48930a40326a2feb4749949151d007cca6c2e6c7fdab9",
      "source_number": "726",
      "mode": "single",
      "status": "PASS",
      "independent_solution": "The required ordered expressions, reused from the independent source freeze, are 5 = log_2(32); -3 = log_3(1/27); 1 = log_12(12); 0 = log_20(1); 1/2 = log_25(5); -1/3 = log_8(1/2). Each supplied power fixes the logarithm's base and argument rather than merely its displayed exponent.",
      "correct": ["A"],
      "option_checks": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "truth": "correct",
          "entry_truths": [true, true, true, true, true, true],
          "evidence": "All six base/argument placements are correct: 2^5 = 32; 3^(-3) = 1/27; 12^1 = 12; 20^0 = 1; 25^(1/2) = 5; 8^(-1/3) = 1/2. No entry is omitted or reordered."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "truth": "incorrect",
          "entry_truths": [false, false, true, false, false, false],
          "evidence": "In order, log_32(2) = 1/5, not 5; log_(1/27)(3) = -1/3, not -3; log_12(12) = 1 is the only correct entry; log_1(20) is undefined because the base is 1; log_5(25) = 2, not 1/2; log_(1/2)(8) = -3, not -1/3. Several reciprocal-looking bases are valid, but their values are wrong."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "truth": "incorrect",
          "entry_truths": [true, false, true, true, true, false],
          "evidence": "Entries 1, 3, 4 and 5 match the required expressions. Entry 2 has log_3(27) = 3, not -3; entry 6 has log_8(2) = 1/3, not -1/3. Dropping the reciprocal arguments changes both signs."
        }
